1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,900
This episode is a practice session, where we translate C code into RISC-V assembly by hand.

2
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:16,652
Let's start by comparing assembly with C.

3
00:00:16,652 --> 00:00:21,619
A C variable has a type, but a register has no type, only thirty-two bits.

4
00:00:21,619 --> 00:00:28,398
In C the type decides the operation, while in assembly the instruction decides how the bits are read.

5
00:00:28,398 --> 00:00:36,600
So if t0 is all ones, a signed instruction sees minus one, and an unsigned one sees over four billion.

6
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,479
Third, a C operator can mean several things.

7
00:00:39,479 --> 00:00:44,650
On line two the plus is pointer arithmetic, on line four it's integer addition,

8
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:48,381
and the two stars are a multiplication and a dereference.

9
00:00:48,381 --> 00:00:54,795
In assembly each instruction name matches exactly one operation, but you count the bytes yourself,

10
00:00:54,795 --> 00:01:00,733
so p plus two moves forward eight bytes, and we write addi with eight.

11
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:07,817
Fourth, one C statement can contain several operations, but a line of assembly holds exactly one instruction.

12
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:11,533
This statement would take about six.

13
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:15,407
Assembly has no variable names either, so comments matter a lot.

14
00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:22,533
Everything from a hash mark to the end of the line is a comment, and there are no multi-line comments.

15
00:01:22,533 --> 00:01:28,638
Let's warm up with f equals the sum of g and h, minus the sum of i and j.

16
00:01:28,638 --> 00:01:34,933
The four inputs live in registers x20 through x23, and f goes into x19.

17
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:38,016
Version A follows the order of the C code.

18
00:01:38,016 --> 00:01:45,137
It computes g plus h into x5, then i plus j into x6, and then subtracts them to get twenty-three.

19
00:01:45,137 --> 00:01:50,133
But that overwrites the values those two registers held before.

20
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:54,397
Version B starts from the same values and uses a little algebra.

21
00:01:54,397 --> 00:01:58,861
It computes g plus h first, then subtracts i, and then subtracts j.

22
00:01:58,861 --> 00:02:05,191
That also gives twenty-three, and it leaves x5 and x6 untouched, but the price is some algebra,

23
00:02:05,191 --> 00:02:12,500
and a more complicated expression, such as one with a multiplication, needs a smarter compiler.

24
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:17,064
There are only thirty-two registers, but that doesn't limit the number of variables,

25
00:02:17,064 --> 00:02:20,533
because whatever doesn't fit gets spilled out to memory.

26
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:27,092
The processor reaches memory through addresses, and the direction is always from the processor's point of view.

27
00:02:27,092 --> 00:02:32,666
Reading data in is called a load, and writing it out is called a store.

28
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:37,380
RISC-V is a load-store architecture, so it computes only on registers.

29
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:43,441
A number in memory is loaded first, and after the calculation it is stored back if needed.

30
00:02:43,441 --> 00:02:49,637
The x86 architecture is different, because an add can take one operand straight from memory,

31
00:02:49,637 --> 00:02:55,800
while RISC-V has to split that into two instructions, an lw and an add.

32
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:59,509
Base plus offset was designed to match arrays and structures.

33
00:02:59,509 --> 00:03:02,003
The base address points at the beginning,

34
00:03:02,003 --> 00:03:08,133
and the offset is a fixed distance in bytes that is known when the code is assembled.

35
00:03:08,133 --> 00:03:13,584
Today's big example has four local variables, and two of them are arrays.

36
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:20,133
We may use only t0, t1, t2, and the stack pointer sp, though memory is free to use.

37
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:26,358
The stack pointer sp is just x2, and it points to the stack that holds the local variables.

38
00:03:26,358 --> 00:03:33,766
That is a convention, and if anyone used x2 as a temporary, every memory access through sp would break.

39
00:03:33,766 --> 00:03:39,492
Where each variable goes is up to us, the human compiler, as long as we stay consistent.

40
00:03:39,492 --> 00:03:42,733
The numbers on the left are offsets from sp.

41
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:45,425
The integer a goes at offset zero from sp.

42
00:03:45,425 --> 00:03:52,026
The array b holds the string plus its terminating null, seven bytes in all, and starts at four from sp.

43
00:03:52,026 --> 00:03:59,717
The array c is ten ints, so forty bytes, covering twelve through fifty-one from sp, and d is a single byte at fifty-two.

44
00:03:59,717 --> 00:04:07,566
Notice that c starts at twelve instead of eleven, so that every int is aligned to a multiple of four.

45
00:04:07,566 --> 00:04:09,896
The first line sets a to five.

46
00:04:09,896 --> 00:04:19,100
Since sw can store only a register's value, we first load five into t0 with li, and then store it at zero from sp.

47
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:25,143
The second line stores the string, and a lookup in the ASCII table gives us its seven bytes.

48
00:04:25,143 --> 00:04:30,792
The most direct way is an li and an sb for each byte, with x0 supplying the null byte,

49
00:04:30,792 --> 00:04:33,600
which comes to thirteen instructions.

50
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:40,618
There's a cleverer way, which is to pack four characters into one word and store it with a single sw.

51
00:04:40,618 --> 00:04:47,566
Because of little-endian order, the first character goes in the lowest byte, so the letters s, t, r,

52
00:04:47,566 --> 00:04:49,790
and i are written as 0x69727473.

53
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:56,700
After the store at four from sp, reading from the low address upward gives exactly s, t, r, i.

54
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:05,442
The remaining n, g, and null get padded with one more zero byte, to make 0x0000676E, which is stored at eight from sp.

55
00:05:05,442 --> 00:05:08,183
That's only four instructions in all.

56
00:05:08,183 --> 00:05:16,703
And because such a big constant doesn't fit in the twelve-bit immediate of addi, li expands into a lui and an addi,

57
00:05:16,703 --> 00:05:19,733
which we'll cover in episode twelve.

58
00:05:19,733 --> 00:05:24,874
The third line, int c of ten, has no initial value, so it needs no instructions,

59
00:05:24,874 --> 00:05:28,466
and the layout itself doesn't generate any either.

60
00:05:28,466 --> 00:05:31,188
The fourth line is d equals b of three.

61
00:05:31,188 --> 00:05:37,261
Since b starts at four from sp, b of three is three bytes further on, at seven from sp.

62
00:05:37,261 --> 00:05:43,125
The lb instruction reads 0x69, which is the letter i, and since its top bit is zero,

63
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:45,638
sign extension fills with zeros too.

64
00:05:45,638 --> 00:05:49,766
Then sb stores it at fifty-two from sp, and that is d.

65
00:05:49,766 --> 00:05:55,442
The fifth line is c of four equals a plus d, and both values have to be loaded first.

66
00:05:55,442 --> 00:06:00,851
The variable a is an int, so we use lw, and d is an unsigned byte, so we use lbu.

67
00:06:00,851 --> 00:06:03,923
Five plus one hundred five is one hundred ten.

68
00:06:03,923 --> 00:06:12,466
Since c starts at twelve from sp, c of four is four times four bytes further on, so we store it at twenty-eight from sp.

69
00:06:12,466 --> 00:06:15,877
The last line, c of a equals twenty, is the hardest.

70
00:06:15,877 --> 00:06:19,353
Can we write a fixed offset, as we did for c of four?

71
00:06:19,353 --> 00:06:23,879
No, because a is a variable that we know to be five only at run time.

72
00:06:23,879 --> 00:06:31,266
An offset has to be fixed when the code is assembled, so the address has to be computed in a register.

73
00:06:31,266 --> 00:06:35,594
The address of c of a is sp plus twelve plus four times a.

74
00:06:35,594 --> 00:06:39,548
First we put twenty into t0, and then load a into t1.

75
00:06:39,548 --> 00:06:47,457
Multiplying by four is a left shift by two, so slli turns binary one zero one into one zero one zero zero,

76
00:06:47,457 --> 00:06:52,000
which is twenty, and we'll cover shifts in episode five.

77
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:00,194
Adding the offset of c, which is twelve, gives thirty-two, or 0x20, and adding the value of sp, 0x1000,

78
00:07:00,194 --> 00:07:02,978
gives the complete address, 0x1020.

79
00:07:02,978 --> 00:07:12,400
Since the address is now complete, the offset is just zero, and sw with zero from t1 writes twenty into c of five.

80
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:13,212
A quick quiz.

81
00:07:13,212 --> 00:07:14,711
What is in t1 right now?

82
00:07:14,711 --> 00:07:19,800
It isn't the value of c of a, but its address.

83
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:22,326
Now for two short exercises.

84
00:07:22,326 --> 00:07:31,260
In the first, x5 holds 0x100, and the question is what x12 contains after these three instructions.

85
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:41,187
The li sets x11 to 0x000093F5, and sw stores it at 0x100 in little-endian order, so the four bytes are F5, 93,

86
00:07:41,187 --> 00:07:43,733
and then two zero bytes.

87
00:07:43,733 --> 00:07:53,166
The lb instruction, with an offset of one from x5, reads the byte at 0x101, which is 0x93 and not 0xF5.

88
00:07:53,166 --> 00:07:58,945
In binary, 0x93 is one zero zero one zero zero one one, so its top bit is a one.

89
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:07,325
The lb instruction does sign extension, which fills the upper twenty-four bits with ones, so x12 becomes 0xFFFFFF93.

90
00:08:07,325 --> 00:08:13,538
That's two traps in one, because little-endian order made the offset of one read 0x93,

91
00:08:13,538 --> 00:08:17,900
and then lb extended it into a negative number.

92
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:22,695
In the second exercise, x and y are int pointers, kept in x3 and x5,

93
00:08:22,695 --> 00:08:27,632
and the question is which instructions implement star x equals star y.

94
00:08:27,632 --> 00:08:36,025
Options one and two copy the pointers themselves, and options three and four use lw to overwrite the pointer registers,

95
00:08:36,025 --> 00:08:38,233
so none of those is right.

96
00:08:38,233 --> 00:08:40,594
The answer is five followed by six.

97
00:08:40,594 --> 00:08:44,506
The lw instruction reads star y into the free register x8,

98
00:08:44,506 --> 00:08:48,689
and then sw writes that value to the address that x points to.

99
00:08:48,689 --> 00:08:52,669
Copying from memory to memory has to go through a register.

100
00:08:52,669 --> 00:08:54,153
The order matters too.

101
00:08:54,153 --> 00:09:00,629
Six then five would store the old value of x8 first, and seven and eight treat x8 as an address,

102
00:09:00,629 --> 00:09:03,366
so they are wrong as well.

103
00:09:03,366 --> 00:09:04,497
Two last details.

104
00:09:04,497 --> 00:09:07,757
The first one is, why is there an lbu but no sbu?

105
00:09:07,757 --> 00:09:10,285
To see why, look at what a store does.

106
00:09:10,285 --> 00:09:16,206
It takes just the lowest byte and puts it into memory as it is, with no extension needed,

107
00:09:16,206 --> 00:09:20,633
whereas a load must fill all thirty-two bits of the register.

108
00:09:20,633 --> 00:09:28,124
A register is just bits, and we can't tell how later instructions will use it, so the upper bits have to be spelled out,

109
00:09:28,124 --> 00:09:32,100
either sign extended or filled with zeros.

110
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,057
The second detail is alignment.

111
00:09:34,057 --> 00:09:41,317
A load or store whose address is a multiple of its access size is guaranteed never to raise a misaligned exception.

112
00:09:41,317 --> 00:09:48,514
What happens with a misaligned access is left to the implementation, since it might be very slow or it might trap,

113
00:09:48,514 --> 00:09:52,300
so treat should be aligned as must be aligned.

114
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:53,430
One more thing.

115
00:09:53,430 --> 00:10:01,766
The spec allows both little-endian and big-endian implementations, and CS61C teaches little-endian.

